  "text": "Question six. Why can a join inflate a sum, and how do you fix it? Joining to a one-to-many table repeats each row once per match. Join the page's twenty-four orders to their line items, to count those too, and each order repeats once per item: forty-eight rows, and the revenue more than doubles. Sara's three hundred four dollars becomes nine hundred twelve, since both her orders have three items. Fix it by aggregating the many side to the right grain first: count the items per order, then join. One row per order, and the revenue is right again. The error multiplies, so a small sample can hide it. Check the row count before and after every join.\n\nQuestion seven. What is a transaction, and how do you use one? It groups statements so they succeed or fail as one: begin, run them, then commit, or roll back. Here's a transfer of thirty. The debit takes account one to seventy. Then recording the transfer fails: account ninety-nine doesn't exist, and the foreign key refuses it. Postgres now refuses every statement until the transaction ends. Roll back, and account one is at a hundred again. The application's job is to catch the error, roll back, and retry or report it, never carry on.",
